w-off (unfriendly?), not good at taking criticism.\n\nB: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, pretentious, sometimes wrong tone, nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers.\n\nC: 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, makes correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, considered moody, regarded as a loner, hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention accuracy, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility/maturity, adaptability/feedback orientation.\n\nWe need to compare candidates.\n\nAgent 2 argued for D, noting his responsibility, problem solving, concentration, adequate stress response, but concerns about temper, moody, loner, know-it-all.\n\nAgent 1 argued for C, noting reliability, positive atmosphere, decision speed, but concerns about communication, egocentric, unwillingness to further education.\n\nWe need to add our perspective, perhaps focusing on a candidate not yet fully discussed: Candidate B's stress handling and conscientiousness vs communication issues. Or we could challenge D's concerns, or argue that C's reliability and decision speed outweigh communication deficits, especially since he puts concern for others above everything and has excellent attention skills.\n\nWe need to bring in explicit facts from our notes. We have for each candidate.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate C's 100% reliability and excellent attention skills, plus decision speed, are critical for long-distance flights, and his concern for others suggests good crew cooperation despite communication difficulty. The egocentric and unwillingness to further education are concerns but may be mitigated.\n\nAlternatively, we could argue that Candidate B's stress handling and conscientiousness are strong, but his weak memory for numbers (attention accuracy) and nasty remarks are serious.\n\nCandidate A lacks evidence for reliability and crew cooperation, plus unorganized and unfriendly.\n\nCandidate D has responsibility and problem solving, but moody, loner, hot temper may impair crew cooperation.\n\nThus, perhaps Candidate C is strongest overall.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 2 raised concerns about C's communication and egocentric attitude; we can argue that his 100% reliability and decision speed are more decisive, and his concern for others and excellent attention skills mitigate communication concerns.\n\nWe need to give a vote: Candidate C.\n\nLet's craft a concise public message: \"I agree with Agent 1 that Candidate C's reliability and decision quality are paramount, and I think his strengths outweigh the communication concerns, especially given his excellent attention skills and concern for others, which support crew cooperation. Therefore I maintain my recommendation for Candidate C.\"\n\nWe must include explicit facts: 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, makes correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything.\n\nExplain why: reliability and decision speed are critical; attention skills support accuracy; concern for others supports crew cooperation; communication difficulty is a concern but less weight than reliability.\n\nThus vote Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
